<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext xml:lang="en-US" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <xi:include href="docinfo.ptx" />
  <book xml:id="algorithms-companion">
    <title>Thinking About Algorithms</title>
    <subtitle>An interactive companion to Jeff Edmonds's How to Think About Algorithms</subtitle>
    <frontmatter xml:id="frontmatter">
      <bibinfo><date>Working draft</date></bibinfo>
      <titlepage><titlepage-items /></titlepage>
      <preface xml:id="start-here">
        <title>Start here</title>
        <p>Design an algorithm, explain why it works, and try to break your explanation. This companion adds short investigations and executable examples to your reading of Jeff Edmonds's <pubtitle>How to Think About Algorithms</pubtitle>.</p>
        <p>Begin with <xref ref="finding-a-maximum" />. You will use a familiar problem to learn what a loop invariant promises and how that promise helps you design code.</p>
        <p>Read a question and commit to a prediction before checking the feedback or running the program. You can work alone or compare explanations with a partner.</p>
        <p>This is an independent companion. The explanations and activities here are newly written; the original book and lectures remain the primary references.</p>
      </preface>
    </frontmatter>
    <chapter xml:id="loop-invariants">
      <title>A promise that survives a loop</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>A long execution can be hard to follow. Instead, describe what one iteration must preserve. We will start with finding a maximum and then use the same reasoning on a different problem.</p>
      </introduction>
      <xi:include href="lesson-01-maximum.ptx" />
    </chapter>
  </book>
</pretext>
